Is each per-part polarity check honest about its evidence? Work out the answer
yourself before reading its verdict. Do not judge whether the board is right:
a reversed part that is flagged passes this test.

For each polarised part, the datasheet research says which footprint pad is
the cathode or pin 1. The built board says which net each physical pad is on.
The intended circuit says which net the cathode or pin 1 must be on. A part is
reversed when the marked pad sits on the wrong net, even if its pin names look
right.

Datasheet research:
{{ ref('research') | select('mapping') | map(attribute='output') | list | tojson(indent=2) }}

Built pad nets:
{{ ref('board').output.pad_nets | tojson(indent=2) }}

Intended pins:
{{ ref('parts').parts | selectattr('polarised') | list | tojson(indent=2) }}

Per-part polarity checks:
{{ ref('polarity') | select('mapping') | list | tojson(indent=2) }}

Fail if a part you found reversed has built_pads_agree true or no issue
naming it, or a part you found correct is flagged reversed. Also fail if a
check's marking is vague ("see datasheet") instead of a physical feature.
Reply with only valid JSON: {"results": "pass"} or {"results": "fail"}.
